const faqItems = [
  { q: "What are the career options after BSc chemistry?", a: "Ten routes are worth comparing: chemistry plus data or teaching content, pharma QC and regulatory work, testing labs, product formulation, tutoring, plant roles, battery and materials, forensic chemistry, research through JAM and NET, and BARC, DRDO or other government labs. A BSc opens the first six directly. The last four mostly name a master's." },
  { q: "Is BSc chemistry a good career?", a: "It is a good base if you add a second skill and keep proof of work. On its own, the BSc leads mostly to entry lab and tutoring roles with modest pay. Payscale shows an average of ₹2,89,512 a year for a chemist role after a BSc. The ceiling rises with method skill, documentation skill, an MSc where a door names it, or a service you run yourself." },
  { q: "Can I get a job with only a BSc in chemistry?", a: "Yes, in QC and testing labs, as a tutor or in formulation support, if the posting accepts a BSc. Read each advertisement, because some name a degree in pharmacy or a master's. Treat entry pay as modest and plan the skill that lifts it." },
  { q: "Is an MSc compulsory after BSc chemistry?", a: "No. It is compulsory for the doors that name it: Food Analyst, Government Analyst, BARC OCES, DRDO Scientist B and NET. It is not compulsory for QC analyst, tutoring or formulation helper jobs. An MSc costs about two years of pay, so take it when a door you want names it." },
  { q: "Which exam should I take after BSc chemistry: JAM, GATE or CSIR NET?", a: "JAM is the usual route into MSc seats at IITs and other institutes. GATE can be taken in the third year of a three-year BSc to seek master's admission at IITs and IISc. CSIR-UGC NET needs a master's, or a four-year bachelor's at 75 percent. Check the official pages of each exam for dates and the Chemistry paper rules." },
  { q: "Can I join BARC or DRDO after BSc chemistry?", a: "Not directly. BARC OCES-2026 asked for an MSc in Chemistry with 60 percent in both the MSc and the BSc, and an age limit of 26 for the general category. DRDO Scientist B is reported to need a first-class master's and a valid GATE Chemistry score. Read the current notice on the official site." },
  { q: "How do I become a food analyst?", a: "You need a master's in Chemistry, Biochemistry, Microbiology, Dairy Chemistry or Food Technology (or a listed equivalent), at least three years of experience analysing food, and a pass in the board FSSAI notifies. A BSc is the first step, not the qualification." },
  { q: "Is analytical chemistry a good career?", a: "It is a steady one. The work is exact, regulated and documented. Growth comes from instrument skill, method validation and moving to QA or regulatory work, not from years served at the same bench. Pay at the start is modest, so look at the ladder, not the first offer." },
  { q: "Is EV battery chemistry a good career in India?", a: "It is a long bet, not a safe first plan. An IEEFA analysis dated January 2026 found that, as of October 2025, only 1.4 GWh of the 50 GWh target (2.8 percent) under the battery cell incentive scheme was commissioned, and the scheme had created 1,118 jobs against an estimate of 1.03 million. Keep a second route running." },
  { q: "Will AI replace chemists?", a: "AI is taking over routine tasks such as report formatting, first-pass calculations and literature searches. A recent review of self-driving labs found most systems only conditionally autonomous, with humans still setting research questions, and no broad evidence yet of AI displacing high-skill research scientists. We found no India-specific job data, so treat this as a reasoned view." },
  { q: "Can a chemistry graduate start a business?", a: "Yes, through tutoring and courses, formulation for small brands, documentation help, or a testing lab. A recognised environmental lab needs at least nine full-time staff under the CPCB procedure, so start smaller and build a track record first." },
];

